<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">James K. Polk</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1795">1795</a>–<a href="/years/1849">1849</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as James Knox Polk</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">James Knox Polk was the 11th president of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, serving from 1845 to 1849. Born in Pineville, North Carolina, a protégé of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andrew-jackson">Andrew Jackson</a> and a Democrat, he became Speaker of the House in 1835 and won the 1844 presidential election as a dark-horse candidate, narrowly defeating Henry Clay. In office he saw <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/texas">Texas</a> join the Union, settled the Oregon dispute with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-great-britain">Great Britain</a> largely along the 49th parallel and led the country to victory in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexican-american-war">Mexican–American War</a>, which brought the cession of the American Southwest. A slaveholder with a plantation in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mississippi-river">Mississippi</a>, he deepened the divide between free and slave states. He served one term, as pledged, and died of cholera in 1849.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/james-k-polk.jpg" alt="James K. Polk" width="330" height="440" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Brady, Mathew B., 1823 (ca.)-1896, photographer.; derivative work by Superwikifan · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:James_K._Polk_restored_(3x4_cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
